{
  "0": "Feature construction: scale each nonpadding input item vector by 1 + 0.1*min(log1p(c), 2), where c counts that item's earlier occurrences in the available causal prefix. This deterministic repeat indicator may expose persistent preferences without adding learned embeddings or an independent scorer. Apply identical prefix-only construction during training and prediction; retain baseline topology, readout, loss and optimizer.",
  "1": "Network architecture: expand each SASRec block's feed-forward intermediate width to four times the hidden dimension, retaining its baseline activation and projecting back to the unchanged hidden size. Greater nonlinear capacity may improve sparse item-context interactions. Preserve attention, embeddings, input features, scoring, readout, loss and optimizer; update the predictor writer to reconstruct the widened blocks.",
  "2": "Sequence modeling: double the baseline maximum context length, retaining the most recent interactions in chronological order and extending the existing positional representation accordingly. Longer causal histories may recover useful preferences lost through baseline truncation. Preserve feature definitions, embedding dimension, Transformer blocks, final-position readout, loss and optimizer; use matching truncation and positions in prediction.",
  "3": "Training strategy: keep the baseline optimizer and peak learning rate, but add linear warmup over epochs 1–10 followed by cosine decay to 10% of that rate at epoch 200. This may stabilize early fitting and reduce late ranking fluctuations visible in the baseline validation traces. Retain baseline batch size, dropout, weight decay, model, loss, seeds and single-checkpoint validation selection."
}
